WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.180
There's a lot about the Crisis on Infinite
Earths crossover spectacular that ultimately

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:10.050
made it a rip-roaring success, but there were
plenty of awkward and inexplicable moments

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.050
as well.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.520
Let's take a look at the five best and worst
moments in Crisis On Infinite Earths.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.910
Turning Oliver Queen into the Spectre, the
universe's agent of justice and vengeance,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.670
was a stroke of genius that many viewers never
saw coming.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:29.050
Episode four of Crisis On Infinite Earths
had a conclusion similar to the comic.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.649
Oliver channeled the energy of his friends,
the Paragons, in order to defeat the Anti-Monitor

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:35.039
and restart the Big Bang.

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:39.790
All of that was less interesting than the
smaller, character-driven moments that Spectre-Oliver

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:43.960
spent with his friends in that episode, as
he guided them through his memories in order

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.399
for them to survive various plot contrivances.

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:52.050
The heart of the Arrowverse has always been
Oliver Queen and Barry Allen's unlikely friendship,

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:57.219
as Oliver drew inspiration from Barry's love
and Barry drew strength from Oliver's resolve.

00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:00.789
The show gave them the closure they needed
and deserved.

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:05.289
In the course of the Arrowverse, when crossovers
get bigger, the story starts to make less

00:01:05.289 --> 00:01:06.289
sense.

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:09.969
In episode one of Crisis, the Monitor tells
the heroes that the last stand will be on

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:12.119
Supergirl's Earth-38.

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:16.770
In subsequent episodes, that gets changed
to Earth-1, as he suddenly tells everyone

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:21.340
to find the Paragons who would help defeat
the Anti-Monitor, who then shows up to destroy

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.110
everything, at which point it becomes all
about defeating the Anti-Monitor at the beginning

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.150
of time.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.960
There's a long digression during which they
try to prevent the Monitor from ever traveling

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.320
through time; finally, the Anti-Monitor's
first defeat doesn't quite stick, so they

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.420
need to find a new way to deal with him by
sending him to the Microverse.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.210
"Essentially we'd be sending him to the 'Atomverse.'"

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.250
"I like to call it the Microverse."

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:44.490
"Oh that's better.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.689
Possible trademark issues, but that's better."

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:51.030
It all adds up to a crossover's worth of MacGuffins,
as it was rarely clear as to what was going

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:55.640
on and why other than a vague sense of the
universe coming to an end.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.710
With a meandering plot and a villain who barely
appeared on the screen, Crisis On Infinite

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:04.479
Earths was saved by the scenery-chewing greatness
of Jon Cryer as Lex Luthor.

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:08.319
It's not just that his Luthor is a smarmy
know-it-all with a vicious streak that's a

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:09.319
delight to watch.

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:14.020
"Never thought I'd say this, but I'm tired
of killing Supermen."

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.790
This Luthor is also a plot-generating machine.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:21.610
Almost every second he's onscreen, he's coming
up with schemes to take advantage of the Crisis

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.510
and punish his enemies Superman and Supergirl.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:28.910
When the multiverse was in danger, Lex steals
the book of destiny in order to kill Superman

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.900
in every reality, leading to some of the best
cameos in the crossover, like Lex confronting

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:38.560
the grown-up Clark Kent from Smallville only
to find out that he had given up his powers.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.000
When going back in time to prevent the Monitor
from accidentally releasing the Anti-Monitor,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.400
Lex tries to manipulate the Monitor instead,
and when the multiverse is rewritten, Lex

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.750
changes things so he's now a hero.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.230
No matter what he did throughout Crisis, Lex
had clear motives, a smart plan, and ruthless

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:55.630
ambition.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:59.740
The Arrowverse shows have always been known
for excellent action set pieces.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.760
What did we get in Crisis On Infinite Earths?

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.280
A bunch of shadow minions that disappeared
on contact.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.450
Even non-powered, non-combatant Dr. Ryan Choi
was able to beat them up with just his two

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.450
fists.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:15.760
The final confrontations with the Anti-Monitor
felt anti-climactic, as those battles wrapped

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.940
up quickly and with a minimum of spectacular
things to look at on screen.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.850
It's enough to make you wish the Anti-Monitor
had summoned various supervillains to be his

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.020
minions, even if only for a scene or two.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:31.180
One of the pleasures of the crossover is seeing
Kate Kane emerge as a significant hero in

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.180
her own right.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.740
In a crossover with a few clever swerves,
the best comes after traveling to Gotham City

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:42.900
on Earth-99 in episode two in order to find
one of the Paragons needed to defeat the Anti-Monitor.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.370
In this case, it's Kevin Conroy, voice of
Batman from the '90s animated series, playing

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:50.820
Batman as an elderly, physically broken and
bitter man.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.210
Supergirl and Batwoman are looking for the
Paragon of Courage, and for a while, it felt

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.960
like their role would be helping Batman regain
his courage, but this Batman is an even darker

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:04.910
version of the character from The Dark Knight
Returns, a stone-cold killer who'd even slain

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:05.910
Superman.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:10.560
"Life only makes sense if you force it to!"

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.890
The real Paragon of Courage turns out to be
Kate Kane herself, who confronts her own demons

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:20.130
by defeating this twisted version of her cousin
in one of the best scenes of the crossover.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:25.140
Tom Cavanagh is usually fun to watch as whatever
version of Harrison Wells appears on The Flash.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.830
His most recent multiverse incarnation, Nash
Wells, seemed to be no exception while he

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.640
was tracking down the Monitor for his own
reasons.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.570
Unfortunately, his motivations became increasingly
incoherent as he seemed to serve just as the

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.990
catalyst who freed the Anti-Monitor.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:44.460
That was bad enough, but his later transformation
into a cosmic being known as the Pariah was

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.160
even more confusing.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.600
His purpose and powers were maddeningly vague,
and he seemed to be little more than an instrument

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.160
to move the plot along.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.090
Even when the world was reset and his memories
of his role in all of this were triggered,

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:01.240
his character didn't do much more than act
as a reminder that they hadn't won just yet.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:06.070
Introducing the noble, stubborn Jefferson
Pierce, a.k.a. Black Lightning, into the Crisis

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:10.260
On Infinite Earths narrative as more than
just a cameo was another smartly-played plot

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.260
swerve.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:15.100
Merging his universe with that of the Flash
and Supergirl means that all of the Arrowverse

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.460
shows are now operating in the same reality,
opening up the possibility of interesting

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.520
guest-star appearances and crossovers in the
future.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.379
His reactions to the other heroes in the course
of the crossover were a highlight.

00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:30.540
From his easy chemistry with Barry Allen,
to being taken aback by seeing two Supermen,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:35.090
who were comic book characters in his reality,
to his wondering aloud about the necessity

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:39.530
of a base, he was a delight, and in the end,
he took his rightful place as part of the

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.260
Justice League.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:46.180
The Anti-Monitor showed no agency or motivations
for his actions other than antimatter and

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.300
matter being unable to coexist in the same
place.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:53.740
What little dialogue he had was mostly the
usual sort of blustery threats from your average

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.830
megalomaniacal super-villain.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:06:00.080
For someone touted as an evil force surpassing
anything the heroes had ever encountered,

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.639
his main evil seemed to be sheer boredom.

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:06.550
His shadow minions were about as effective
and terrifying as paper ghosts.

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.990
Worst of all, he just seemed kind of dumb.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:13.270
He seemed so certain of his victory that he
didn't seem to have any real plans or schemes

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:17.090
that the heroes had to deal with, nor did
he care to stop the heroes from doing what

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.030
they were doing.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.970
The final scenes of episode five were note-perfect.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:25.970
Beginning with Barry Allen and Sara Lance
eulogizing their friend Oliver Queen and setting

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:30.040
up an eternal flame for him to the old Super
Friends theme playing over the final reveal

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:35.419
of the Hall of Justice, the show leaned heavily
on DC Universe mythology and nostalgia.

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:39.350
With Oliver gone, it's only right that Barry
should be the new catalyst for the heroes,

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:43.530
and it's fitting that for the hero designated
as the Paragon of Love, his idea was a big

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.690
clubhouse where his friends could hang out.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:49.660
The sheer look of glee on the actors' faces
when they sat at the round table of this new

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:55.200
Justice League was palpable, and it opens
up a multitude of storytelling possibilities.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.020
The only character who died in the crossover
was Oliver Queen, and it took him a couple

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.110
of tries before he finally perished.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:05.169
The Arrowverse has never been shy about killing
characters, so it may seem odd that in an

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:09.940
event as big as this there would be so few
permanent casualties, although it's easy to

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:14.150
understand this decision from a storytelling
perspective; the writers clearly wanted to

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:18.890
keep as many characters around as possible
for the revised realities of their TV shows.

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:22.610
Thematically, it was also clear that they
wanted to honor the Green Arrow and actor

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:24.840
Stephen Amell, as Arrow is ending.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.530
Having too many characters sacrificing themselves
would have taken away from the focus on Oliver.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:33.400
That said, it did feel like a cheat that pretty
much every significant character wound up

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:37.240
just fine in the end, even if their realities
were shuffled with a bit.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:40.840
The heroes were supposed to be confronting
the end of everything, and the outcome didn't

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.060
reflect this.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.139
Hopefully, future crossovers will be scaled
back a bit.

